    },
    {
      "id": "crane-crash",
      "text": "In the mid-1800s perhaps 1,500 lived in North America. By 1941, fifteen wintered in Texas.",
      "sourceIDs": ["icf-whooping-crane-species", "audubon-whooping-crane"]
    },
    {
      "id": "crane-thread",
      "text": "The species survived on one long thread: a 2,500-mile migration between wetlands in northern Canada and the Texas coast.",
      "sourceIDs": ["icf-texas-program", "parks-canada-wood-buffalo-cranes"]
    },
    {
      "id": "crane-hidden",
      "text": "Its nesting grounds were so remote they were not even discovered until 1954.",
      "sourceIDs": ["parks-canada-wood-buffalo-cranes", "audubon-whooping-crane"]
    },
    {
      "id": "crane-ultralight",
      "text": "Recovery was slow, strange, and human: captive breeding, and a Wisconsin flock that learned to migrate by following an ultralight aircraft.",
      "sourceIDs": ["icf-wisconsin-reintroduction"]
    },
    {
      "id": "crane-climb",
      "text": "The wild flock has climbed past five hundred, its highest count since near-extinction.",
      "sourceIDs": ["parks-canada-wood-buffalo-cranes"]
    },
    {
      "id": "crane-threats",
      "text": "The dangers now are power lines, illegal shooting, and upstream water use that starves the coast of the blue crabs the cranes winter on.",
      "sourceIDs": ["icf-north-america-program", "icf-texas-program"]
